{
  "dom_id": "step-q34722",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Calculate the second derivative of  $f(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4s⟩$ with respect to $s.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the derivative of $f(s)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(s)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
